      <h1 id="scaling-a-user-provisioned-cluster-with-the-bare-metal-operator" class="group scroll-mt-20">Scaling a user-provisioned cluster with the Bare Metal Operator<a href="#scaling-a-user-provisioned-cluster-with-the-bare-metal-operator" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h1>
      <p>After deploying a user-provisioned infrastructure cluster, you can use the Bare Metal Operator (BMO) and other metal^3^ components to scale bare-metal hosts in the cluster. This approach helps you to scale a user-provisioned cluster in a more automated way.</p>
      <h2 id="about-scaling-a-user-provisioned-cluster-with-the-bare-metal-operator_scaling-a-user-provisioned-cluster-with-the-bare-metal-operator" class="group scroll-mt-20">About scaling a user-provisioned cluster with the Bare Metal Operator<a href="#about-scaling-a-user-provisioned-cluster-with-the-bare-metal-operator_scaling-a-user-provisioned-cluster-with-the-bare-metal-operator" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>You can scale user-provisioned infrastructure clusters by using the Bare Metal Operator (BMO) and other metal^3^ components. User-provisioned infrastructure installations do not feature the Machine API Operator. The Machine API Operator typically manages the lifecycle of bare-metal nodes in a cluster. However, it is possible to use the BMO and other metal^3^ components to scale nodes in user-provisioned clusters without requiring the Machine API Operator.</p>
      <h3 id="upi-prequisites-for-scaling-a-upi-cluster_scaling-a-user-provisioned-cluster-with-the-bare-metal-operator" class="group scroll-mt-20">Prerequisites for scaling a user-provisioned cluster<a href="#upi-prequisites-for-scaling-a-upi-cluster_scaling-a-user-provisioned-cluster-with-the-bare-metal-operator" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <ul>
      <li>You installed a user-provisioned infrastructure cluster on bare metal.</li>
      <li>You have baseboard management controller (BMC) access to the hosts.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 id="upi-limitations-for-scaling-a-upi-cluster_scaling-a-user-provisioned-cluster-with-the-bare-metal-operator" class="group scroll-mt-20">Limitations for scaling a user-provisioned cluster<a href="#upi-limitations-for-scaling-a-upi-cluster_scaling-a-user-provisioned-cluster-with-the-bare-metal-operator" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <ul>
      <li>
      You cannot use a provisioning network to scale user-provisioned infrastructure clusters by using the Bare Metal Operator (BMO).
      <ul><li>Consequentially, you can only use bare-metal host drivers that support virtual media networking booting, for example <code>redfish-virtualmedia</code> and <code>idrac-virtualmedia</code>.</li></ul>
      </li>
      <li>You cannot scale <code>MachineSet</code> objects in user-provisioned infrastructure clusters by using the BMO.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="configuring-a-provisioning-resource-to-scale-user-provisioned-clusters_scaling-a-user-provisioned-cluster-with-the-bare-metal-operator" class="group scroll-mt-20">Configuring a provisioning resource to scale user-provisioned clusters<a href="#configuring-a-provisioning-resource-to-scale-user-provisioned-clusters_scaling-a-user-provisioned-cluster-with-the-bare-metal-operator" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>Create a <code>Provisioning</code> custom resource (CR) to enable Metal platform components on a user-provisioned infrastructure cluster.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul><li>You installed a user-provisioned infrastructure cluster on bare metal.</li></ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a <code>Provisioning</code> CR.
      <ol>
      <li>
      Save the following YAML in the <code>provisioning.yaml</code> file:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: metal3.io/v1alpha1\nkind: Provisioning\nmetadata:\n  name: provisioning-configuration\nspec:\n  provisioningNetwork: \"Disabled\"\n  watchAllNamespaces: false"} lang="yaml" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>OpenShift Container Platform 4.22 does not support enabling a provisioning network when you scale a user-provisioned cluster by using the Bare Metal Operator.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the <code>Provisioning</code> CR by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f provisioning.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"provisioning.metal3.io/provisioning-configuration created"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      Verify that the provisioning service is running by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get pods -n openshift-machine-api"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                                                  READY   STATUS    RESTARTS        AGE\ncluster-autoscaler-operator-678c476f4c-jjdn5          2/2     Running   0               5d21h\ncluster-baremetal-operator-6866f7b976-gmvgh           2/2     Running   0               5d21h\ncontrol-plane-machine-set-operator-7d8566696c-bh4jz   1/1     Running   0               5d21h\nironic-proxy-64bdw                                    1/1     Running   0               5d21h\nironic-proxy-rbggf                                    1/1     Running   0               5d21h\nironic-proxy-vj54c                                    1/1     Running   0               5d21h\nmachine-api-controllers-544d6849d5-tgj9l              7/7     Running   1 (5d21h ago)   5d21h\nmachine-api-operator-5c4ff4b86d-6fjmq                 2/2     Running   0               5d21h\nmetal3-6d98f84cc8-zn2mx                               5/5     Running   0               5d21h\nmetal3-image-customization-59d745768d-bhrp7           1/1     Running   0               5d21h"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="upi-provisioning-new-hosts-in-a-upi-cluster_scaling-a-user-provisioned-cluster-with-the-bare-metal-operator" class="group scroll-mt-20">Provisioning new hosts in a user-provisioned cluster by using the BMO<a href="#upi-provisioning-new-hosts-in-a-upi-cluster_scaling-a-user-provisioned-cluster-with-the-bare-metal-operator" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>You can use the Bare Metal Operator (BMO) to provision bare-metal hosts in a user-provisioned cluster by creating a <code>BareMetalHost</code> custom resource (CR).</p>
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>Provisioning bare-metal hosts to the cluster by using the BMO sets the <code>spec.externallyProvisioned</code> specification in the <code>BareMetalHost</code> custom resource to <code>false</code> by default. Do not set the <code>spec.externallyProvisioned</code> specification to <code>true</code>, because this setting results in unexpected behavior.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You created a user-provisioned bare-metal cluster.</li>
      <li>You have baseboard management controller (BMC) access to the hosts.</li>
      <li>You deployed a provisioning service in the cluster by creating a <code>Provisioning</code> CR.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a configuration file for the bare-metal node. Depending if you use either a static configuration or a DHCP server, choose one of the following example <code>bmh.yaml</code> files and configure it to your needs by replacing values in the YAML to match your environment:
      <ul>
      <li>
      To deploy with a static configuration, create the following <code>bmh.yaml</code> file:
      <CodeBlock code={"---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Secret\nmetadata:\n  name: openshift-worker-<num>-network-config-secret (1)\n  namespace: openshift-machine-api\ntype: Opaque\nstringData:\n  nmstate: | (2)\n    interfaces: (3)\n    - name: <nic1_name> (4)\n      type: ethernet\n      state: up\n      ipv4:\n        address:\n        - ip: <ip_address> (5)\n          prefix-length: 24\n        enabled: true\n    dns-resolver:\n      config:\n        server:\n        - <dns_ip_address> (6)\n    routes:\n      config:\n      - destination: 0.0.0.0/0\n        next-hop-address: <next_hop_ip_address> (7)\n        next-hop-interface: <next_hop_nic1_name> (8)\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Secret\nmetadata:\n  name: openshift-worker-<num>-bmc-secret\n  namespace: openshift-machine-api\ntype: Opaque\ndata:\n  username: <base64_of_uid> (9)\n  password: <base64_of_pwd>\n---\napiVersion: metal3.io/v1alpha1\nkind: BareMetalHost\nmetadata:\n  name: openshift-worker-<num>\n  namespace: openshift-machine-api\nspec:\n  online: true\n  bootMACAddress: <nic1_mac_address> (10)\n  bmc:\n    address: <protocol>://<bmc_url> (11)\n    credentialsName: openshift-worker-<num>-bmc-secret\n    disableCertificateVerification: false\n  customDeploy:\n    method: install_coreos\n  userData:\n    name: worker-user-data-managed\n    namespace: openshift-machine-api\n  rootDeviceHints:\n    deviceName: <root_device_hint> (12)\n  preprovisioningNetworkDataName: openshift-worker-<num>-network-config-secret"} lang="yaml" />
      <ol>
      <li>Replace all instances of <code>&lt;num&gt;</code> with a unique compute node number for the bare-metal nodes in the <code>name</code>, <code>credentialsName</code>, and <code>preprovisioningNetworkDataName</code> fields.</li>
      <li>Add the NMState YAML syntax to configure the host interfaces. To configure the network interface for a newly created node, specify the name of the secret that has the network configuration. Follow the <code>nmstate</code> syntax to define the network configuration for your node. See "Preparing the bare-metal node" for details on configuring NMState syntax.</li>
      <li>Optional: If you have configured the network interface with <code>nmstate</code>, and you want to disable an interface, set <code>state: up</code> with the IP addresses set to <code>enabled: false</code>.</li>
      <li>Replace <code>&lt;nic1_name&gt;</code> with the name of the bare-metal node’s first network interface controller (NIC).</li>
      <li>Replace <code>&lt;ip_address&gt;</code> with the IP address of the bare-metal node’s NIC.</li>
      <li>Replace <code>&lt;dns_ip_address&gt;</code> with the IP address of the bare-metal node’s DNS resolver.</li>
      <li>Replace <code>&lt;next_hop_ip_address&gt;</code> with the IP address of the bare-metal node’s external gateway.</li>
      <li>Replace <code>&lt;next_hop_nic1_name&gt;</code> with the name of the bare-metal node’s external gateway.</li>
      <li>Replace <code>&lt;base64_of_uid&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;base64_of_pwd&gt;</code> with the base64 string of the user name and password.</li>
      <li>Replace <code>&lt;nic1_mac_address&gt;</code> with the MAC address of the bare-metal node’s first NIC. See the "BMC addressing" section for additional BMC configuration options.</li>
      <li>Replace <code>&lt;protocol&gt;</code> with the BMC protocol, such as IPMI, Redfish, or others. Replace <code>&lt;bmc_url&gt;</code> with the URL of the bare-metal node’s baseboard management controller.</li>
      <li>Optional: Replace <code>&lt;root_device_hint&gt;</code> with a device path when specifying a root device hint. See "Root device hints" for additional details.</li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      When configuring the network interface with a static configuration by using <code>nmstate</code>, set <code>state: up</code> with the IP addresses set to <code>enabled: false</code>:
      <CodeBlock code={"---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Secret\nmetadata:\n  name: openshift-worker-<num>-network-config-secret\n  namespace: openshift-machine-api\n # ...\ninterfaces:\n  - name: <nic_name>\n    type: ethernet\n    state: up\n    ipv4:\n      enabled: false\n    ipv6:\n      enabled: false\n# ..."} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      To deploy with a DHCP configuration, create the following <code>bmh.yaml</code> file:
      <CodeBlock code={"---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Secret\nmetadata:\n  name: openshift-worker-<num>-bmc-secret (1)\n  namespace: openshift-machine-api\ntype: Opaque\ndata:\n  username: <base64_of_uid> (2)\n  password: <base64_of_pwd>\n---\napiVersion: metal3.io/v1alpha1\nkind: BareMetalHost\nmetadata:\n  name: openshift-worker-<num>\n  namespace: openshift-machine-api\nspec:\n  online: true\n  bootMACAddress: <nic1_mac_address> (3)\n  bmc:\n    address: <protocol>://<bmc_url> (4)\n    credentialsName: openshift-worker-<num>-bmc\n    disableCertificateVerification: false\n  customDeploy:\n    method: install_coreos\n  userData:\n    name: worker-user-data-managed\n    namespace: openshift-machine-api\n  rootDeviceHints:\n    deviceName: <root_device_hint> (5)"} lang="yaml" />
      <ol>
      <li>Replace <code>&lt;num&gt;</code> with a unique compute node number for the bare-metal nodes in the <code>name</code> and <code>credentialsName</code> fields.</li>
      <li>Replace <code>&lt;base64_of_uid&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;base64_of_pwd&gt;</code> with the base64 string of the user name and password.</li>
      <li>Replace <code>&lt;nic1_mac_address&gt;</code> with the MAC address of the bare-metal node’s first NIC. See the "BMC addressing" section for additional BMC configuration options.</li>
      <li>Replace <code>&lt;protocol&gt;</code> with the BMC protocol, such as IPMI, Redfish, or others. Replace <code>&lt;bmc_url&gt;</code> with the URL of the bare-metal node’s baseboard management controller.</li>
      <li>
      Optional: Replace <code>&lt;root_device_hint&gt;</code> with a device path when specifying a root device hint. See "Root device hints" for additional details.
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>If the MAC address of an existing bare-metal node matches the MAC address of the bare-metal host that you are attempting to provision, then the installation will fail. If the host enrollment, inspection, cleaning, or other steps fail, the Bare Metal Operator retries the installation continuously. See "Diagnosing a duplicate MAC address when provisioning a new host in the cluster" for additional details.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the bare-metal node by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f bmh.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"secret/openshift-worker-<num>-network-config-secret created\nsecret/openshift-worker-<num>-bmc-secret created\nbaremetalhost.metal3.io/openshift-worker-<num> created"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Inspect the bare-metal node by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc -n openshift-machine-api get bmh openshift-worker-<num>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold">&lt;num&gt;</dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the compute node number.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                    STATE       CONSUMER   ONLINE   ERROR\nopenshift-worker-<num>  provisioned true"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Approve all certificate signing requests (CSRs).
      <ol>
      <li>
      Get the list of pending CSRs by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get csr"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME        AGE   SIGNERNAME                                    REQUESTOR                                         REQUESTEDDURATION CONDITION\ncsr-gfm9f   33s   kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client-kubelet   system:serviceaccount:openshift-machine-config-o\nperator:node-bootstrapper   <none>              Pending"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Approve the CSR by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc adm certificate approve <csr_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"certificatesigningrequest.certificates.k8s.io/<csr_name> approved"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      Verify that the node is ready by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get nodes"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME        STATUS   ROLES           AGE     VERSION\napp1        Ready    worker          47s     v1.24.0+dc5a2fd\ncontroller1 Ready    master,worker   2d22h   v1.24.0+dc5a2fd"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Additional resources</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li><a href="/openshift-docs-markdown/installing/installing_bare_metal/bare-metal-expanding-the-cluster#preparing-the-bare-metal-node_bare-metal-expanding">Preparing the bare-metal node</a></li>
      <li><a href="/openshift-docs-markdown/installing/installing_bare_metal/ipi/ipi-install-installation-workflow#root-device-hints_ipi-install-installation-workflow">Root device hints</a></li>
      <li><a href="/openshift-docs-markdown/installing/installing_bare_metal/bare-metal-expanding-the-cluster#ipi-install-diagnosing-duplicate-mac-address_bare-metal-expanding">Diagnosing a duplicate MAC address when provisioning a new host in the cluster</a></li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="upi-managing-existing-hosts-in-a-upi-cluster_scaling-a-user-provisioned-cluster-with-the-bare-metal-operator" class="group scroll-mt-20">Optional: Managing existing hosts in a user-provisioned cluster by using the BMO<a href="#upi-managing-existing-hosts-in-a-upi-cluster_scaling-a-user-provisioned-cluster-with-the-bare-metal-operator" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>Optionally, you can use the Bare Metal Operator (BMO) to manage existing bare-metal controller hosts in a user-provisioned cluster by creating a <code>BareMetalHost</code> object for the existing host. It is not a requirement to manage existing user-provisioned hosts; however, you can enroll them as externally-provisioned hosts for inventory purposes.</p>
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>To manage existing hosts by using the BMO, you must set the <code>spec.externallyProvisioned</code> specification in the <code>BareMetalHost</code> custom resource to <code>true</code> to prevent the BMO from re-provisioning the host.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You created a user-provisioned bare-metal cluster.</li>
      <li>You have baseboard management controller (BMC) access to the hosts.</li>
      <li>You deployed a provisioning service in the cluster by creating a <code>Provisioning</code> CR.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create the <code>Secret</code> CR and the <code>BareMetalHost</code> CR.
      <ol>
      <li>
      Save the following YAML in the <code>controller.yaml</code> file:
      <CodeBlock code={"---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Secret\nmetadata:\n  name: controller1-bmc\n  namespace: openshift-machine-api\ntype: Opaque\ndata:\n  username: <base64_of_uid>\n  password: <base64_of_pwd>\n---\napiVersion: metal3.io/v1alpha1\nkind: BareMetalHost\nmetadata:\n  name: controller1\n  namespace: openshift-machine-api\nspec:\n  bmc:\n    address: <protocol>://<bmc_url> (1)\n    credentialsName: \"controller1-bmc\"\n  bootMACAddress: <nic1_mac_address>\n  customDeploy:\n    method: install_coreos\n  externallyProvisioned: true (2)\n  online: true\n  userData:\n    name: controller-user-data-managed\n    namespace: openshift-machine-api"} lang="yaml" />
      <ol>
      <li>You can only use bare-metal host drivers that support virtual media networking booting, for example <code>redfish-virtualmedia</code> and <code>idrac-virtualmedia</code>.</li>
      <li>You must set the value to true to prevent the BMO from re-provisioning the bare-metal controller host.</li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the bare-metal host object by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f controller.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"secret/controller1-bmc created\nbaremetalhost.metal3.io/controller1 created"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      Verify that the BMO created the bare-metal host object by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get bmh -A"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAMESPACE               NAME          STATE                    CONSUMER   ONLINE   ERROR   AGE\nopenshift-machine-api   controller1   externally provisioned              true             13s"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="upi-removing-hosts-from-a-upi-cluster_scaling-a-user-provisioned-cluster-with-the-bare-metal-operator" class="group scroll-mt-20">Removing hosts from a user-provisioned cluster by using the BMO<a href="#upi-removing-hosts-from-a-upi-cluster_scaling-a-user-provisioned-cluster-with-the-bare-metal-operator" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>You can use the Bare Metal Operator (BMO) to remove bare-metal hosts from a user-provisioned cluster.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You created a user-provisioned bare-metal cluster.</li>
      <li>You have baseboard management controller (BMC) access to the hosts.</li>
      <li>You deployed a provisioning service in the cluster by creating a <code>Provisioning</code> CR.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Cordon and drain the node by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc adm drain app1 --force --ignore-daemonsets=true"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"node/app1 cordoned\nWARNING: ignoring DaemonSet-managed Pods: openshift-cluster-node-tuning-operator/tuned-tvthg, openshift-dns/dns-\ndefault-9q6rz, openshift-dns/node-resolver-zvt42, openshift-image-registry/node-ca-mzxth, openshift-ingress-cana\nry/ingress-canary-qq5lf, openshift-machine-config-operator/machine-config-daemon-v79dm, openshift-monitoring/nod\ne-exporter-2vn59, openshift-multus/multus-additional-cni-plugins-wssvj, openshift-multus/multus-fn8tg, openshift\n-multus/network-metrics-daemon-5qv55, openshift-network-diagnostics/network-check-target-jqxn2, openshift-ovn-ku\nbernetes/ovnkube-node-rsvqg\nevicting pod openshift-operator-lifecycle-manager/collect-profiles-27766965-258vp\nevicting pod openshift-operator-lifecycle-manager/collect-profiles-27766950-kg5mk\nevicting pod openshift-operator-lifecycle-manager/collect-profiles-27766935-stf4s\npod/collect-profiles-27766965-258vp evicted\npod/collect-profiles-27766950-kg5mk evicted\npod/collect-profiles-27766935-stf4s evicted\nnode/app1 drained"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Delete the <code>customDeploy</code> specification from the <code>BareMetalHost</code> CR.
      <ol>
      <li>
      Edit the <code>BareMetalHost</code> CR for the host by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc edit bmh -n openshift-machine-api <host_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Delete the lines <code>spec.customDeploy</code> and <code>spec.customDeploy.method</code>:
      <CodeBlock code={"...\n  customDeploy:\n    method: install_coreos"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify that the provisioning state of the host changes to <code>deprovisioning</code> by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get bmh -A"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAMESPACE               NAME          STATE                    CONSUMER   ONLINE   ERROR   AGE\nopenshift-machine-api   controller1   externally provisioned              true             58m\nopenshift-machine-api   worker1       deprovisioning                      true             57m"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Delete the host by running the following command when the <code>BareMetalHost</code> state changes to <code>available</code>:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc delete bmh -n openshift-machine-api <bmh_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>You can run this step without having to edit the <code>BareMetalHost</code> CR. It might take some time for the <code>BareMetalHost</code> state to change from <code>deprovisioning</code> to <code>available</code>.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      Delete the node by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc delete node <node_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
